      trivia: "The brand name — quorum, “the number required to constitute a meeting” — is, by the company’s account, a pun: the minimum outlay that constitutes a proper cigar. Its parent, J.C. Newman, is a venerable house covering the full span of prices from the premium Diamond Crown down to this budget Quorum — among America’s oldest family cigar firms, begun in 1895 in a one-man barn workshop and now in its fourth generation. The PENSA factory in Estelí that carries the bulk of production was built by the Newman brothers themselves in 2011 and is known as an environmentally minded plant with rainwater harvesting and natural lighting. Though a volume brand of which it is said that “one in every eight Nicaraguan cigars imported into the United States is a Quorum”, it remains hand-rolled and chiefly Nicaraguan in leaf — the point of difference, so the telling goes, from the machine-made cheap cigars of its price class."
